The solution uses a dynamic-programming table over item suffixes and capacities. It is tied to a recursive specification function `Best`, and two lemmas prove the two postconditions. I have not run this through Dafny 4.11.0 here, so it is not machine-checked yet.

- **`Best(ws, vs, c)`** is the standard unbounded-knapsack recurrence. For the first item, it takes the larger of two options:
  - skip the item: `Best(ws[1..], vs[1..], c)`
  - take one more copy: `vs[0] + Best(ws, vs, c - ws[0])`
- **`UpperBound`** shows that any valid strategy's value is at most `Best`. It works by induction on the item count and then on `counts[0]`.
- **`ExistsOpt`** builds a strategy whose value is exactly `Best`.
- **The method** fills a 1-D array, one item at a time from the last item back to the first. After each pass, `row[j] == Best(weights[i..], values[i..], j)` for every capacity `j`.

