response:
The proof does not use exchange arguments. It counts components at each weight threshold.

- **Algorithm.** Component labels are kept in a `seq<int>`, and a merge relabels every node. Thresholds `t` go from −100000 to 100000. At each `t`, every adjacency entry with weight `t` is scanned, so no sorting is needed. An edge is added when its endpoints have different labels.
- **Ghost history.** After each threshold `t`, the method records the labeling `hist[t]`. Every graph edge with weight ≤ `t` has endpoints with the same label in `hist[t]`. The number of chosen edges with weight ≤ `t` equals `n − NumLabels(hist[t])`.
- **Any other spanning tree.** Start from `hist[t]` and merge along only its edges heavier than `t`. Each merge removes at most one label. Because the tree spans, the labels end up all equal, so it has at least `NumLabels(hist[t]) − 1` heavy edges. So for every `t`, it has no more edges of weight ≤ `t` than Kruskal's result.
- **Weights.** `tw(S,t) = Σ max(w,t)` satisfies `tw(S,t+1) = tw(S,t) + cntLe(S,t)`. Working down from `t = 100000` gives `total_weight(T) ≤ total_weight(O)`.

