This uses exhaustive search rather than Gaussian elimination, so it runs in exponential time (2^n candidates for n columns). It is practical only for small inputs, even though the contracts allow up to 100 columns. I chose it because the correctness proof is short and reliable, especially for the `None` case. A fully verified Gaussian elimination would need a much longer proof: that row operations preserve the solution set, plus reasoning about the reduced row echelon form.

